- `ValueKind` is always `Element`. `IsEmptyElement` says how an element with no children is written: as an empty-element tag (`<e/>`) when it's `true`, which is the default for an element you create, and as `<e></e>` when it's `false`. A parsed element has the value its source had, and `DeepClone` copies it. The property isn't read while the element has children; such an element is written with an end tag.
- `XmlWriterOptions.EmptyElementStyle` sets the form of the one tag, `<e/>` or `<e />`. With `StartAndEndTag`, every element with no children is written as `<e></e>`, whatever `IsEmptyElement` is.
- The one-argument indexer matches a local name in any namespace. `SetAttribute(name, value)` sets the attribute in no namespace. The overloads that take a namespace URI match that namespace only; pass an empty string for no namespace.
- The indexers return `null` when nothing matches. They search direct children only.
- `SetAttribute` stores a `null` value as an empty string. It doesn't remove the attribute.
- Setting `InnerText` removes every child, including child elements and comments, and adds one text node. Setting it to `null` or an empty string leaves the element empty.
- `LocalName` rejects `null`, an empty string, and white space when it's set. Whether the name is a valid XML name is checked only when the element is written.
- The element's namespace comes from its `NamespaceUri`. When the element is written, a namespace declaration attribute for the element's own prefix is written with the element's namespace, even if the attribute's value says something else.
- A prefix with no namespace can't be written; `ToString` and `WriteTo` throw an `XmlException` with the message "Prefixed names require a namespace URI.".
- `XmlElementNode` implements `IEnumerable<XmlNode>` over `Children`, so `foreach` and LINQ work on it directly. Don't change `Children` while you enumerate it: the loop's next step throws an `InvalidOperationException`. Loop over a copy (`Children.ToArray()`), or loop by index from the end.
- An element of a tree from `XmlNode.Parse` or `ParseValue` makes its attributes and children the first time you reach into it, for example through `Children`, `Attributes`, or `InnerText`, or by changing it. Until then it's written and copied from the parsed input. Nothing you read or write depends on whether that has happened.
